       },
       {
        "text": "I'm using **nf-core/methylseq** for this. Both samples are single-end RRBS bisulfite libraries, and this pipeline gives a methylation level for every CpG it covers in each sample.\n\n**What I built so far**\n- One samplesheet with 2 samples: D.SEQ-261028BMC-15 (NTC-METH-1) and D.SEQ-261028BMC-16 (NTC-METH-2). Both are 75 bp single-end reads on a HiSeq 4000.\n- Settings: Bismark aligner, `rrbs: true` (MspI-digested material, so duplicate removal is skipped) and `cytosine_report: true` (a full per-cytosine report). The pipeline's parameter check passed.\n\n**What this answers, and what it doesn't**\nThe run gives you methylation at each CpG, not promoter values. To get those, you'd average the CpG calls over a promoter set, such as the region around each gene's start (for example \u00b11\u20132 kb). That step happens after the run and isn't part of it.\n\n**Reference genome is still missing**\nNothing in these samples' records says what species they are, so no genome is set yet. Bismark also needs its own bisulfite index of that genome on Luria before the run can start. This is the only thing blocking the run.\n\n**Run time:** there's no estimate for this pipeline yet.\n\n**Which organism are these samples from?** You can name a species (\"human\", \"mouse\") or a specific genome version (e.g. GRCh38, GRCm39). Once I have that I'll set the reference and show you the final config to confirm. When the run finishes, just tell Nessie \"my run is done\" and Nessie will turn the outputs into NExtSEEK upload workbooks for you to review.",
        "type": "text"
       }
      ],
